  "step1_summary": "- Chief complaint: Fever and night sweats for 7 days.\n- Symptoms (onset/duration/severity/aggravating or alleviating factors):\n  - Fever for ~7 days with intermittent hot and cold sensations and night sweats.\n  - Generalised myalgia/aching all over.\n  - Nausea with reduced oral intake; low fluid intake.\n  - Tiredness/fatigue and unwellness affecting ability to work.\n  - Intermittent baseline headaches (chronic, screen-related) but nothing new/severe.\n  - Took OTC paracetamol and ibuprofen at recommended doses with only partial symptomatic relief.\n  - No cough, sore throat, ear pain, diarrhoea, dysuria, neck stiffness, rash, or focal joint swelling.\n- Recent exposures: Travel to Vietnam; returned 4 weeks ago. Multiple rural/countryside exposures (trekking, cycling through farmland). Possible freshwater/river exposure not clearly stated.\n- Past medical history: Chronic back pain (under physiotherapy); long-standing headaches/migraines related to screen use.\n- Medications / allergies:\n  - Regular medication: Seroxat (paroxetine).\n  - Recent OTC: paracetamol and ibuprofen.\n  - Allergy: pollen (hay fever). No drug allergies reported.\n  - Malaria prophylaxis: Patient reports taking malaria prophylaxis while travelling (thought it was daily and \"another one on top of the first one\") but exact drug(s), adherence, and completeness unknown.\n- Family / social history: Father died of myocardial infarction (~age 68). Patient is a nurse, non-smoker, moderate alcohol intake, exercises moderately.\n- Vital signs / exam findings: No vitals or physical exam findings documented in the transcript.\n- Labs / imaging: No labs/imaging performed yet. Doctor plans clinic exam and blood tests including thick and thin blood films for malaria and inflammatory markers; urgency depends on exam.\n",
